// Distributed schedule for example 18-multigather — the load-bearing
// schedule for the TASK-0389.01 FIFO-ordering residual.
//
// Two workers:
//   host  -- effectful I/O (load_a, save_pq). Owns the file system.
//   w0    -- the pure compute loop body (`add`). Produces BOTH `p` and
//            `q` inside `for i`.
//
// Cross-worker dataflow edges (one `transfer` per crossing symbol):
//   a (host -> w0)   produced by load_a  on host, consumed by add on w0.
//   p (w0 -> host)   produced inside for i on w0, consumed by save_pq.
//   q (w0 -> host)   produced inside for i on w0, consumed by save_pq.
//
// `p` and `q` are BOTH loop outputs on the SAME (w0 -> host) channel:
// their host-side Push is hoisted out past the `for i` Repeat (the
// `splice_pushes_global` cut branch). `p` is produced before `q`
// (statement order), so producer-rank order is [p, q]. The worker waits
// {p, q} (rank-sorted by `build_waits_for_op`); the host must therefore
// SEND {p, q} on the channel. TASK-0389.01 makes the co-hoisted Push
// textual (= wire-send) order equal producer-rank order, so the
// strict-FIFO `wire::read_msg_expect` pairs by construction. Before the
// fix the naive splice reversed the co-hoisted Pushes (host sent {q, p})
// and this cell failed LOUD on mp-tcp-bufsync / mp-tcp-poll.
//
// All `sync` — the minimum semantic every tier-1 backend offers. No
// loop transforms, no partition (a single `w0` runs the whole loop; the
// point is two outputs on ONE channel, not many workers).

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0 };

    // I/O on host.
    place load_a  on host;
    place save_pq on host;

    // Compute loop body on the single compute worker.
    place add on w0;

    // Cross-worker transfers (PRD §6.3.4: a crossing edge with no
    // transfer directive is a hard error, not a silent default).
    transfer a : sync;
    transfer p : sync;
    transfer q : sync;
}
